{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789774167,"nonce":"u8ZNOMWJB+WNQWHBid+GIWo9WIc=","title":"Cranelift Load and Store treated a single-scalar struct as bytes at an address","message":"A struct of one scalar field is carried as that scalar, in a register\n(abi::struct_carried_as_its_field), and insertvalue/extractvalue on it\nalready move the scalar. Load and Store of such a struct still took the\naggregate path: Load allocated a stack slot and copied 8 bytes to it,\nhanding the slot's address on as the value, and Store read 8 bytes from\nthe scalar's bits as if they were an address.\n\nReproducer: `One { x: f64 }` stored to an alloca and loaded back, then\n`.x` read: the address of the copy instead of 2.5, or a fault. A tuple of\none element in a Python program is such a struct.\n\nFix: Load and Store decide by the shared `hir_interp::held` rule, so\nonly aggregates held by reference are copied. Covered by the scalar-struct\ncases in `crates/compiler/tests/nested_aggregate_fields.rs`.","files":null}]}